  "image_name": "93410901e0027b8bc9289c015c9e4490.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several DNA-based strategies for programming self-assembly with high specificity and directional control. Overall, it shows how DNA can encode both the identity of building blocks and the way they fit together, allowing complex target structures and tunable assembly pathways.\n\nOne theme is that highly specific “DNA bricks” can assemble many distinct components into a prescribed 3D object, illustrated by a multicolored block structure labeled “Big Ben.” A related example shows DNA-grafted particles organizing into an ordered, rod-like aggregate. Another theme is shape complementarity: matching geometries on DNA origami-like components, or on colloidal particles with complementary curved surfaces, create selective, reversible interactions that favor particular orientations rather than random sticking.\n\nThe figure also highlights hybrid approaches that combine DNA origami with colloids to produce emergent directional bonds. These include polyhedral frames with particle-like nodes and “DNA stick frame” coatings around spheres, both suggesting ways to impose valence-like connectivity.\n\nFinally, the bottom-right section shows DNA strand displacement as a kinetic control tool. By programming zero, one, or two displacement reactions, the assembly pathway and phase behavior can be shifted, changing whether the system remains fluid, forms a solid, or shows coexistence.",
